# aws s3 cp exclude list — CVM → S3 upload (cvm-pull.sh uses this)
# run/rollout.jsonl is intentionally NOT excluded: it is the new-pilot cost and
# incident source. usage.json remains an optional legacy compatibility file.
# Per-experiment browser installations are disposable runtime caches. Preserve
# browser/version evidence, not hundreds of MiB of duplicated Chromium bytes.
run/playwright/*
run/playwright-browsers/*
*/run/playwright/*
*/run/playwright-browsers/*
work/playwright/*
work/playwright-browsers/*
*/work/playwright/*
*/work/playwright-browsers/*
#
# NB (2026-07-29): aws --exclude matches the RELATIVE path from source
# root, not the absolute path. `*/.foo/*` requires a leading `/` component
# and does NOT match `.foo/x` at the top of the source. Since exp dirs
# are the source root, top-level dirs need the un-prefixed form. Both
# forms are listed so nested occurrences (rare) are also caught.
.codex/*
*/.codex/*
stderr.log
*/stderr.log
__pycache__/*
*/__pycache__/*
# bwrap overlay bookkeeping — v4 runner collects the unique rollout into
# $EXP_DIR/run/rollout.jsonl. What's left is sqlite state, plugin locks,
# and socket/FIFO tmp files that aws warns on. Default cvm-pull now preserves
# failed/postmortem CVM dirs instead of excluding this state and deleting it.
.codex-upper/*
*/.codex-upper/*
.codex-work/*
*/.codex-work/*
# Preserve per-exp Git commits, objects, index, and refs: they are canonical
# Workspace authority. Only duplicated LFS payload caches are disposable.
.git/lfs/*
*/.git/lfs/*
